WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.553
How does arc welding work?

2
00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:05.982
On the welding table lie two steel plates.

3
00:00:06.532 --> 00:00:08.900
Arc welding melts their edges together.

4
00:00:09.451 --> 00:00:12.589
When the metal sets, they are one piece.

5
00:00:13.489 --> 00:00:15.579
The heat comes from electricity.

6
00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:22.703
The power source gives only 20 to 35 volts, but 100 to 400 amps.

7
00:00:23.253 --> 00:00:25.041
A kettle draws about 10.

8
00:00:25.941 --> 00:00:31.504
Between the wire and the steel an arc jumps across: a bright, continuous spark.

9
00:00:32.055 --> 00:00:33.982
That is where the metal melts.

10
00:00:34.882 --> 00:00:39.343
A spool pushes the wire through the hose to the welding gun, the torch.

11
00:00:39.893 --> 00:00:43.309
As the wire melts away, new wire keeps coming.

12
00:00:44.209 --> 00:00:47.471
And gas flows from the bottle to the tip of the torch.

13
00:00:48.671 --> 00:00:50.947
This is MIG/MAG welding.

14
00:00:51.497 --> 00:00:57.061
The wire, 0.8 to 1.2 millimetres thick, is the electrode,

15
00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:00.302
and melts too: that is how it fills the groove.

16
00:01:01.202 --> 00:01:04.348
Under the arc lies a glowing puddle: the weld pool.

17
00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:07.673
Behind the arc it freezes into the weld.

18
00:01:08.573 --> 00:01:11.475
Air causes gas bubbles and a brittle weld.

19
00:01:12.025 --> 00:01:14.463
So shielding gas flows from the nozzle.

20
00:01:15.014 --> 00:01:18.929
In MAG, for steel, argon with some carbon dioxide.

21
00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:22.256
In MIG, for aluminium, pure argon.

22
00:01:23.456 --> 00:01:25.859
TIG welding works differently.

23
00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:29.381
The electrode is tungsten and does not melt.

24
00:01:29.931 --> 00:01:33.925
With the other hand the welder dips a separate rod into the weld pool.

25
00:01:34.825 --> 00:01:40.562
Often three to five times slower, but clean and precise: for stainless pipework,

26
00:01:40.763 --> 00:01:42.667
thin sheet and aluminium.

27
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:47.071
The arc energy is the heat per millimetre of weld.

28
00:01:47.621 --> 00:01:53.882
At 24 volts, 220 amps and 400 millimetres per minute,

29
00:01:54.082 --> 00:01:57.762
that is 0.79 kilojoules per millimetre.

30
00:01:58.662 --> 00:02:05.187
In MAG, 0.8 of that goes into the workpiece: a heat input of 0.63.

31
00:02:05.737 --> 00:02:10.300
Inside the 0.5 to 0.9 of the welding procedure.

32
00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.833
The steel beside the weld got hot and changed: the HAZ, the heat-affected zone.

33
00:02:17.383 --> 00:02:20.018
Too much heat makes it wider and weaker.

34
00:02:20.569 --> 00:02:23.239
Too little, and the weld does not fuse.

35
00:02:24.439 --> 00:02:28.793
The wire feed speed is how many metres of wire go in each minute.

36
00:02:29.343 --> 00:02:31.433
More wire draws more current.

37
00:02:32.333 --> 00:02:36.234
Set low, the wire keeps dipping into the pool: the short arc.

38
00:02:36.784 --> 00:02:38.943
Little heat, good for thin sheet.

39
00:02:39.843 --> 00:02:44.324
Set high, a fine stream of droplets flies across: the spray arc.

40
00:02:44.874 --> 00:02:47.869
Deep penetration, but only flat or horizontal.

41
00:02:48.769 --> 00:02:53.517
The deposition rate is how many kilos of weld metal are added per hour.

42
00:02:54.067 --> 00:03:00.522
Wire of 1.2 millimetres at 10 metres per minute: about 5.3 kilos an hour.

43
00:03:01.722 --> 00:03:04.694
Behind a welding robot the gas hose kinks.

44
00:03:05.244 --> 00:03:08.634
The flow drops from 15 to 5 litres per minute.

45
00:03:09.534 --> 00:03:12.239
Within an hour the welds are full of pores.

46
00:03:13.139 --> 00:03:17.272
The fix: a sensor stops the robot below 10 litres per minute.

47
00:03:18.472 --> 00:03:21.235
A weld must also be right on paper.

48
00:03:21.785 --> 00:03:27.418
First comes the pWPS: the recipe you want to test, on a test piece.

49
00:03:28.318 --> 00:03:33.037
Each essential variable, such as material, gas or thickness,

50
00:03:33.237 --> 00:03:35.187
has a range of qualification.

51
00:03:35.737 --> 00:03:38.840
Step outside it, and you must test again.

52
00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:44.500
A test plate of 12 millimetres covers 3 to 24 millimetres.

53
00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:47.476
So 22 millimetres is covered.

54
00:03:48.376 --> 00:03:54.810
Tested at 1.2 kilojoules per millimetre, with impact requirements up to 1.5.

55
00:03:55.361 --> 00:03:57.706
So 1.6 is outside.

56
00:03:58.606 --> 00:04:03.262
The welder holds a certificate to ISO 9606-1.

57
00:04:03.812 --> 00:04:06.768
The welding coordinator oversees which welder

58
00:04:06.768 --> 00:04:09.396
and which procedure belong to each weld.

59
00:04:10.596 --> 00:04:12.105
The weld cools down.

60
00:04:12.655 --> 00:04:17.241
t8/5 is the time from 800 to 500 degrees.

61
00:04:18.141 --> 00:04:24.161
If it is short, martensite forms: hard, brittle steel that cracks easily.

62
00:04:25.061 --> 00:04:28.730
The carbon equivalent says how quickly a steel hardens.

63
00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:33.901
A CEV of 0.46 on 40 millimetres is high.

64
00:04:35.101 --> 00:04:39.350
So preheat, and measure the interpass temperature before every pass.

65
00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:43.036
A hydrogen crack can appear days later.

66
00:04:43.586 --> 00:04:47.197
Inspection often waits 16 to 48 hours.

67
00:04:48.097 --> 00:04:53.521
On stainless steel the heat tint beside the weld must come off, or it rusts there.
